WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:31.010
When you peer into the microcosmos, it’s
sometimes hard to see past the cells themselves.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:37.330
Flitting around, fighting, surviving, movement
and shapes and colors.

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:43.990
But we can see deeper, and there is so much
to see if you look beyond that first level,

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.240
and into the cell itself.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.820
In the cytoplasm, mysteries and curiosities
abound.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.400
Here in our macro-world,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.160
to see inside an organism we need expensive scans or exploratory surgeries.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:08.220
In the microcosmos, we can just turn on the
light and see some of the bizarre and beautiful

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:17.250
systems that these organisms have for increasing
their chances of surviving a harsh and uncertain world.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.220
So let’s take a look at some of the structures
inside of cells that you may have never

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.440
heard of, and that we love to let blow our
minds.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.960
This is the unicellular alga Closterium.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.860
And at the very tips of the cells, an alluring
little object.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:46.050
Do you see that orb with the tiny round things
inside?

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:52.280
Those are actual crystals made of barium and
calcium sulfate.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.960
But why are they there?

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:03.530
Well, no one knows yet, and we hope...nay
we know... someone will someday solve this mystery.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:09.740
But for now, it is yet another thing that we do
not know about our universe.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:14.960
These crystals are extremely small.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.880
Around two to three microns across.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:25.120
If you divided a milimeter into 500 equal
pieces, one of those pieces

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.050
is how big those little grains are.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.250
But there’s more.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:37.030
You see how those crystals jiggle around and
never stop moving, even though nothing seems

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.560
to be moving them.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:45.480
Well, one thing we know about the universe
is that things don’t just move….the energy

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.940
has to be coming from somewhere.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:52.640
Let’s remember that everything you're looking at here is made of atoms.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.750
And when two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen
atom come together, they form a water molecule.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:07.220
Now, Closterium lives in water, which means
it's surrounded by and filled with water molecules.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:15.560
And they are in constant motion, that’s what heat is. The movement of atoms and molecules.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.380
If they weren’t moving at all, that would
be absolute zero.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:24.000
-273.15 degrees Celcius.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.700
That's not where we're at.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.920
These water molecules are moving around a lot and they're bumping into
everything and

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.540
they can even move objects around.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:40.160
But if something is really big, like a whole
Closterium cell, you can't actually see any movement.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:46.870
But since these crystals are tiny, the water molecules visibly toss them around.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:52.640
This dancing is called Brownian
motion, and we love it because we

00:03:52.640 --> 00:04:00.550
of course cannot see water molecules under the microscope, but we can observe their effect

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.310
on these crystals.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:09.130
Indeed, Albert Einstein used brownian motion
to confirm that atoms and molecules exist

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:15.700
which, in 1905 when he published his paper
on the subject, was still a matter of debate!

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:22.740
And now, we’re watching it happen...watching
water molecules smashing around into crystals

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.060
that, as yet, remain unexplained,

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:33.920
but must have some reason to exist besides being fun for us to look at.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.099
This is Nasulla ornata, a beautiful unicellular
ciliate.

00:04:44.099 --> 00:04:48.340
Although it doesn’t look like it right now,
they can move quite fast.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:52.749
But what you are looking at is a squashed Nassula ornata.

00:04:52.749 --> 00:04:57.540
We decided to give it a bit of a squeeze so we could take a real good look.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.060
When Nassula ornata hunts for food,

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.080
It prefers filamentous cyanobacteria or algae

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.620
It wraps its membrane around it, forming a food vacuole.

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:13.099
As it continues to eat it forms more and more of these vacuoles.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.460
Now, what's so nice about them are their colors.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.760
Cyanobacteria and algae, Nassula’s food,
are green,

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.620
but when Nassula starts dumping digestive enzymes into these food vacuoles,

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:32.400
a chemical reaction occurs changing
the color of the food vacuole over time.

00:05:32.409 --> 00:05:38.229
Because different vacuoles are at different
stages of digestion, each vacuole is a slightly

00:05:38.229 --> 00:05:43.260
different color, giving this ciliate its beautiful, colored polka dots.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:52.860
These are the very long, very skinny cells of the filamentous sulfur bacteria Beggiatoa.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:59.620
You can find them all over the globe living
in habitats from marine caves to sulfur springs

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:02.639
to everyday ponds and rivers.

00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:10.129
When we find them, they are usually on freshwater
pond sediments or decomposing organic material.

00:06:10.129 --> 00:06:15.920
Beggiatoa excretes mucus, which allows them to glide around on the sediments.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:23.479
Beggiatoa prefers sulfur-rich environments
because they oxidize hydrogen sulfide as an

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:24.949
energy source.

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:31.389
The hydrogen is used by the organism in chemical synthesis, and the sulfur is left over.

00:06:31.389 --> 00:06:38.309
Every one of those tiny black dots that you see is a granule of inorganic sulfur.

00:06:38.309 --> 00:06:46.300
Here’s another clip where you can look at
them a bit closer under 1000 times magnification.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.640
Which, for the moment, is about as good as we can do here.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:58.260
Now, what’s happening here...these little
bubbles forming and popping...well they are

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:04.339
not bubbles of air, they’re bubbles of pure
water.

00:07:04.339 --> 00:07:10.620
One of the most fundamental structures for
life as a freshwater single-celled organism

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.360
is the contractile vacuole.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.880
This organelle allows unicellular organisms
to pump excess water from their cell.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.280
Why?

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.979
Well, Inside of every cell, there are a lot
of dissolved substances—more than the surrounding

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:28.550
water contains, certainly.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:33.680
Whenever there is an imbalance like this, things
tend toward equilibrium.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:39.159
And the only thing separating these cells
from the water around them is a membrane that

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:44.660
allows some, but not all substances
to pass through it passively.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:47.660
One of those substances is water.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:52.760
When the concentration of stuff inside the
cell is higher than the concentration of stuff

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:57.880
outside, water diffuses into the cell.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:01.839
There is no way to stop this diffusion.

00:08:01.839 --> 00:08:08.660
The solution to this constant influx of water
is the contractile vacuole.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:16.229
This organelle fights a continuous battle, pumping and pumping and pumping the water out.

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:21.009
Imagine, though, if the contractile vacuole
was not working.

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:25.839
You might think that, eventually, the cell
would fill with so much water that the cellular

00:08:25.839 --> 00:08:28.030
machinery might break down.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:36.140
Well, maybe, except, before that happened,
the cell would expand so much

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:40.800
that its membrane would break and it would literally explode.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:43.240
So, y’know, good job little vacuole.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:54.740
The ciliate Loxodes is a common resident of
different aquatic habitats, but they prefer

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:57.560
a distinct zone in the water.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:08:59.880
A “Goldilocks” zone for oxygen.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:04.180
They like concentration not to be too
high and not too low.

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:10.390
And in a body water, unless something weird is
going on, the oxygen concentration is higher at

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:14.530
the top and lower toward the bottom.

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:20.200
Because of its specific oxygen concentration
preference, Loxodes needs to know which way

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:25.310
is up and which way is down so it can find
just the right spot.

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:30.500
But how could a single celled organism know
up from down?

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:35.600
Well, Loxodes can sense gravity!

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:42.820
It has organelles called Müller vesicles
that each contain a spherical mineral granule—the

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:47.170
Müller body—attached to a hair-like cilium.

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:51.560
Sorry these friends zip around so quick, it
can be hard to get a good look.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:56.560
These granules, the Müller bodies are actually pulled down by gravity,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.700
and it is believed that this “pulling”

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:03.600
causes sensory signals to be transmitted to
the cell through the cilium.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:11.540
So, Loxodes, despite being tiny and despite being just a single cell, has an organ very

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:17.260
much like our inner ear, allowing it to sense
up from down.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:24.700
So yes, microbes display quite the collection
of intracellular structures!

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.950
Only a few of which we discussed here.

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:34.097
Some shiny, some colorful, and some that provide
fascinating advanced capabilities.

00:10:34.097 --> 00:10:35.800
And it makes us wonder,

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:42.840
what other structures are yet to be discovered? There is so much that we do not know.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:48.640
We hope to report back to you on this question
because we are sure that there are many more

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:56.660
out there just waiting to be unveiled by a
discerning eye peering into the microcosmos.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:05.380
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:08.380
check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:12.680
And if you want to see more from us here at Journey to the Microcosmos,

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.320
I bet there's a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

